  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Bava A.K vs Food Inspector on 23 February, 2016 Author: B.Sudheendra Kumar Bench: B.Sudheendra Kumar In The High Court Of Kerala At Ernakulam Present: The Honourable Mr. Justice B.Sudheendra Kumar Tuesday, The 23Rd Day Of February 2016/4Th Phalguna, 1937 Crl.Rev.Pet.No. 2642 of 2009 ( ) --------------------------------- Crl.A 372/2007 of Addl. Sessions Court (Adhoc)-I, Kasaragod Stc 7/2004 of Chief Judicial Magistrate Court, Kasaragod Revision Petitioner(S)/Appellants/Accused: ----------------------------------------- 1. Bava A.K., Aged 33 Years, S/O.Muhammed, Kalander Store X-1279, Kumbala, Residing At Nangi House Kasaragode. 2. B.N.Muhammed Ali, Aged 48 Years, S/O.M.P.Muhammed Haji, Licencee Kalander Store, X-1279, Kumbala Bus Stand Shopping Complex, Kasaragode. By Adv. Sri.T.G.Rajendran Respondent(S)/Complainant & State: ---------------------------------- 1. Food Inspector, Kasaragode Circle, Kumbala Grama Panchayath. 2. State Of Kerala, Rep.By Public Prosecutor, High Court Of Kerala Ernakulam. Bava A.K vs Food Inspector on 23 February, 2016 Indian Kanoon - http://indiankanoon.org/doc/131658748/ 1 R, By Public Prosecutor Sri.R.Githesh This Criminal Revision Petition Having Been Finally Heard On 23-02-2016, The Court On The Same Day Passed The Following: Scl. B. Sudheendra Kumar, J. ------------------------------------- Crl.R.P.No. 2642 of 2009 -------------------------------------- Dated this the 23rd day of February, 2016 Order",
    "facts": "The first accused in S.T.No.7 of 2004 on the files of the Court of the Chief Judicial Magistrate, Kasaragod, has filed this revision petition challenging the concurrent finding of conviction and sentence passed by the courts below under Sections 16 (1) (a) (i), 7 (i) and 2 (ia) (m) of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Act, 1954 and Rule 47 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, 1955. The prosecution allegation can be briefly stated thus:- On 12.08.2004, PW1, the Food Inspector purchased two sealed identical packets of sweet supari (Madhu) containing 50 pouches from the first accused. The second accused was the licensee. After sampling as per rules, one sample was sent to the Public Analyst for analysis. The Public Analyst, after analysis submitted Ext.P11 report stating that the sample contained 11947.8 parts per million of sodium saccharin and is therefore adulterated as per Rule 47 of the Prevention of Food Adulteration Rules, 1955. 4. Before the trial Court, PW1 to PW4 were examined and Exts.P1 to P28 were marked for the prosecution. Ext.D1 was marked for the revision petitioners.",
    "arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P11 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument. It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioners and the learned Public Prosecutor.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "5. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners has submitted that since no laboratory for the analysis of the food item was defined and no validated method of analysis was prescribed as per the mandate of Section 23 (1-A)(ee) and (hh) of the PFA Act, Ext.P11 cannot be acted upon to hold that the food item purchased by the Food Inspector was adulterated. The learned counsel for the revision petitioners relied on the decision of the Apex Court in Pepsico India Holdings (P) Ltd. v. Food Inspector [(2011) 1 SCC 176] to buttress his argument.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "It was held by the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) that since the Laboratories were not notified and the tests conducted by such laboratories were not admissible in evidence, no prosecution could be based on such report and the accused was entitled to get acquittal. In the decision of this Court in Gopalakrishnan v. Food Inspector [2013 (3) KLT 455], the court relied on the decision of the Supreme Court in Pepsico India Holdings (supra) and found that the Public Analyst's report was not admissible in evidence and accordingly, the Court acquitted the accused. The matter was referred to a Division Bench and the Division Bench also affirmed the said decision, submitted by the learned counsel for the petitioners and the learned Public Prosecutor."
  },
